„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙšAnd when they bring you unto the synagogues, and unto magistrates, and powers, take ye no thought how or what thing ye shall answer, or what ye shall say:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙIfor the Holy Ghost shall teach you in the same hour what ye ought to say.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses™„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjAnd one of the company said unto him, Master, speak to my brother, that he divide the inheritance with me.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙEAnd he said unto him, Man, who made me a judge or a divider over you?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‘And he said unto them, Take heed, and beware of covetousness: for a man's life consisteth not in the abundance of the things which he possesseth.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙeAnd he spake a parable unto them, saying, The ground of a certain rich man brought forth plentifully:¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙiand he thought within himself, saying, What shall I do, because I have no room where to bestow my fruits?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ~And he said, This will I do: I will pull down my barns, and build greater; and there will I bestow all my fruits and my goods.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙxAnd I will say to my soul, Soul, thou hast much goods laid up for many years; take thine ease, eat, drink, and be merry.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙŒBut God said unto him, Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee: then whose shall those things be, which thou hast provided?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙISo is he that layeth up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦versesš„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ—And he said unto his disciples, Therefore I say unto you, Take no thought for your life, what ye shall eat; neither for the body, what ye shall put on.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ>The life is more than meat, and the body is more than raiment.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙConsider the ravens: for they neither sow nor reap; which neither have storehouse nor barn; and God feedeth them: how much more are ye better than the fowls?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙFAnd which of you with taking thought can add to his stature one cubit?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙYIf ye then be not able to do that thing which is least, why take ye thought for the rest?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ™Consider the lilies how they grow: they toil not, they spin not; and yet I say unto you, that Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ›If then God so clothe the grass, which is to day in the field, and to morrow is cast into the oven; how much more will he clothe you, O ye of little faith?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙZAnd seek not ye what ye shall eat, or what ye shall drink, neither be ye of doubtful mind.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙwFor all these things do the nations of the world seek after: and your Father knoweth that ye have need of these things.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTBut rather seek ye the kingdom of God; and all these things shall be added unto you.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book)§chapter«first_verse ªlast_verse"¦verses“„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVFear not, little flock; for it is your Father's good pleasure to give you the kingdom.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ­Sell that ye have, and give alms; provide yourselves bags which wax not old, a treasure in the heavens that faileth not, where no thief approacheth, neither moth corrupteth.¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ:For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.¦layout ªlast_verse"…¤book)§chapter«first_verse#ªlast_verse(¦verses–„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ8Let your loins be girded about, and your lights burning;¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¥and ye yourselves like unto men that wait for their lord, when he will return from the wedding; that when he cometh and knocketh, they may open unto him immediately.¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÇBlessed are those servants, whom the lord when he cometh shall find watching: verily I say unto you, that he shall gird himself, and make them to sit down to meat, and will come forth and serve them.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsAnd if he shall come in the second watch, or come in the third watch, and find them so, blessed are those servants.¦layout ªlast_verse&„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¦And this know, that if the goodman of the house had known what hour the thief would come, he would have watched, and not have suffered his house to be broken through.¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSBe ye therefore ready also: for the Son of man cometh at an hour when ye think not.¦layout ªlast_verse(…¤book)§chapter«first_verse)ªlast_verse0¦verses˜„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙSThen Peter said unto him, Lord, speakest thou this parable unto us, or even to all?¦layout ªlast_verse)„¥verse*¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ£And the Lord said, Who then is that faithful and wise steward, whom his lord shall make ruler over his household, to give them their portion of meat in due season?¦layout ªlast_verse*„¥verse+¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙJBlessed is that servant, whom his lord when he cometh shall find so doing.¦layout ªlast_verse+„¥verse,¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙMOf a truth I say unto you, that he will make him ruler over all that he hath.¦layout ªlast_verse,„¥verse-¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¤But and if that servant say in his heart, My lord delayeth his coming; and shall begin to beat the menservants and maidens, and to eat and drink, and to be drunken;¦layout ªlast_verse-„¥verse.¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÀthe lord of that servant will come in a day when he looketh not for him, and at an hour when he is not aware, and will cut him in sunder, and will appoint him his portion with the unbelievers.¦layout ªlast_verse.„¥verse/¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd that servant, which knew his lord's will, and prepared not himself, neither did according to his will, shall be beaten with many stripes.¦layout ªlast_verse/„¥verse0¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙæBut he that knew not, and did commit things worthy of stripes, shall be beaten with few stripes. For unto whomsoever much is given, of him shall be much required: and to whom men have committed much, of him they will ask the more.¦layout ªlast_verse0…¤book)§chapter«first_verse1ªlast_verse5¦verses•„¥verse1¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOI am come to send fire on the earth; and what will I, if it be already kindled?¦layout ªlast_verse1„¥verse2¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙZBut I have a baptism to be baptized with; and how am I straitened till it be accomplished!¦layout ªlast_verse2„¥verse3¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWSuppose ye that I am come to give peace on earth? I tell you, Nay; but rather division:¦layout ªlast_verse3„¥verse4¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙgfor from henceforth there shall be five in one house divided, three against two, and two against three.¦layout ªlast_verse4„¥verse5¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙôThe father shall be divided against the son, and the son against the father; the mother against the daughter, and the daughter against the mother; the mother in law against her daughter in law, and the daughter in law against her mother in law.¦layout ªlast_verse5…¤book)§chapter«first_verse6ªlast_verse;¦verses–„¥verse6¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ‚And he said also to the people, When ye see a cloud rise out of the west, straightway ye say, There cometh a shower; and so it is.¦layout ªlast_verse6„¥verse7¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙWAnd when ye see the south wind blow, ye say, There will be heat; and it cometh to pass.¦layout ªlast_verse7„¥verse8¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙsYe hypocrites, ye can discern the face of the sky and of the earth; but how is it that ye do not discern this time?¦layout ªlast_verse8„¥verse9¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ;Yea, and why even of yourselves judge ye not what is right?¦layout ªlast_verse9„¥verse:¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙøWhen thou goest with thine adversary to the magistrate, as thou art in the way, give diligence that thou mayest be delivered from him; lest he hale thee to the judge, and the judge deliver thee to the officer, and the officer cast thee into prison.¦layout ªlast_verse:„¥verse;¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRI tell thee, thou shalt not depart thence, till thou hast paid the very last mite.¦layout ªlast_verse;